arm64: dts: h713: EXPERIMENT -- allocate scanout from a carveout, not CMA

EXPERIMENT, not a fix. Keep it out of `series` except while running it.

Something writes pixel-shaped data over kernel memory whenever mpv renders
on the GPU into a CMA-backed scanout buffer. The evidence for that shape is
a 2x2 (docs/vaapi-scope.md):

  GPU + carveout scanout  clean, 480 s      (run D, gles-play)
  GPU + CMA scanout       crash, 40-90 s    (n=3)
  no GPU + CMA scanout    clean, 2406 s / 143,646 commits

Neither ingredient is sufficient alone. But the clean carveout arm used
gles-play, so it changed the renderer AND the memory source at once, and
the two have never been separated. This patch separates them: same mpv,
same panfrost, same KMS scanout, same everything -- only the memory the
scanout buffer comes from changes.

Reading the code first did not find the writer. panfrost's import path is
sound (mappings are krefed and torn down, imported pages are pinned by the
dma_buf reference, get_pgsize() cannot over-map), the CMA-page-migration
idea is dead (an allocated CMA buffer is out of the buddy allocator, and
drm_gem_shmem_create() deliberately drops __GFP_MOVABLE), and patch 0053 --
which fixed a genuine cacheability mismatch on exactly these buffers --
made no difference to the crash schedule. Notably there has never been a
single panfrost MMU fault, so the GPU is not escaping its page tables.

The pool is 64 MiB and the size is derived, not guessed. A shared-dma-pool
is a coherent pool, so dma_alloc_from_dev_coherent() allocates in
power-of-two page orders: mesa's scanout buffer measures 3,723,264 bytes
(/sys/kernel/debug/dma_buf/bufinfo, four of them attached to 1800000.gpu),
which takes a 4 MiB slot. 64 MiB is 16 slots against the four mpv's
swapchain uses plus one held by the fbcon console. The attempt recorded in
patch 0038 failed for exactly this reason and no other -- 16 MiB gave four
slots, fbcon took one, and mpv died at CREATE_DUMB with ENOMEM.

`no-map` is the point, not an incidental: it keeps the region out of the
page allocator entirely, so these pages are never recycled into slab or
stacks, which is what makes this arm differ from CMA in the way that
matters.

Placement is clear of everything, checked against /proc/iomem on the
running board: above the 8 MiB uboot-scanout at 0x6c100000, above the
0x6d000000 where U-Boot stages bootlogo.bmp (which is pre-Linux only), and
below the 128 MiB CMA the kernel reserves at 0x76c00000.

Expected outcomes, both useful:

  - clean for well past 90 s -> the CMA-ness of the scanout buffer is
    necessary, with the renderer held constant. That makes a carveout a
    real candidate shipping configuration, and narrows the writer to
    something that only misbehaves against recyclable memory.
  - crashes on the old schedule -> the memory source is NOT the variable,
    run D was clean for some other reason, and the difference is in
    gles-play versus mpv/mesa.

Signed-off-by: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
---
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi
@@ -115,6 +115,31 @@
 			reg = <0x6c100000 0x800000>;
 			no-map;
 		};
+
+		/*
+		 * EXPERIMENT (docs/vaapi-scope.md): scanout memory that is not
+		 * CMA. Something scribbles pixel-shaped data over kernel memory
+		 * whenever panfrost renders into a CMA-backed scanout buffer, and
+		 * the one arm that has ever been clean with the GPU running took
+		 * its scanout from a reserved carveout. This makes that the ONLY
+		 * difference, with mpv and the GPU otherwise identical.
+		 *
+		 * 64 MiB, and the size is chosen not guessed. A shared-dma-pool
+		 * allocates in power-of-two page orders, so mesa's 3,723,264-byte
+		 * scanout buffer (measured, /sys/kernel/debug/dma_buf/bufinfo)
+		 * occupies a 4 MiB slot: 16 slots here, against the four mpv's
+		 * swapchain uses plus one for the fbcon console. The earlier
+		 * attempt at this failed only because 16 MiB gave exactly four.
+		 *
+		 * Placement is clear of everything: above uboot-scanout, above
+		 * the 0x6d000000 where U-Boot stages bootlogo.bmp (pre-Linux
+		 * only), and below the 128 MiB CMA the kernel puts at 0x76c00000.
+		 */
+		afbd_pool: framebuffer-pool@70000000 {
+			compatible = "shared-dma-pool";
+			reg = <0x70000000 0x4000000>;
+			no-map;
+		};
 	};
 
 	psci {
@@ -1672,6 +1697,14 @@
 			clocks = <&ccu CLK_AFBD>,
 				 <&ccu CLK_BUS_DISP>;
 			clock-names = "clk_afbd", "clk_bus_disp";
+			/*
+			 * EXPERIMENT. This deliberately contradicts the note on
+			 * uboot-scanout above, which records that handing this
+			 * driver a dma pool was tried and abandoned. That was a
+			 * sizing failure at 16 MiB, not a design verdict; the
+			 * pool here is 64 MiB. Revert both hunks together.
+			 */
+			memory-region = <&afbd_pool>;
 			status = "okay";
 		};
 
